                <h2>This Week’s Shift: Compliance Is Moving Beyond Creative Reviews</h2>
<p>The key development this week is not a single new regulation, but the continued expansion of platform review practices. Reviewers increasingly examine whether the business identity, landing-page domain, payment method, and ad content tell the same story. Even a creative without an obvious violation may face delayed review if supporting information is incomplete or inconsistent.</p>
<p>This trend has a greater impact on advertisers managing multiple accounts. The more accounts a team operates, the more likely it is for naming conventions, agency authorization, payment currencies, and reused creatives to diverge.</p>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team ran several ad accounts but reused creatives across country-specific stores. During a routine inspection, the team found that advertised prices, inventory levels, and promotion deadlines did not match the landing pages. The ads were removed, and several accounts had to submit new creatives for review.</p>
<p><strong>Weekly recommendation:</strong> Do not wait for a rejection before checking compliance. Move from reviewing individual ads to validating the entire evidence chain.</p>
<h2>A Seven-Step Weekly Compliance Inspection SOP</h2>
<h3>1. Verify account ownership and business identity</h3>
<p>Maintain a centralized account register showing account IDs, legal entity, target country, platform role, onboarding date, agency representative, and payment account. Check whether:</p>
<ul>
<li>Business registration documents remain valid;</li>
<li>The entity displayed on the account matches the paying entity;</li>
<li>Agencies have current authorization covering the accounts they manage;</li>
<li>Access rights and shared email accounts are revoked when employees leave.</li>
</ul>
<p>Entity information is the foundation for every subsequent review. If it is inconsistent, checking creatives alone is unlikely to resolve the issue.</p>
<h3>2. Reconcile recharges and payment evidence</h3>
<p>Link recharge records, invoices, and payment confirmations to individual ad accounts. A balance screenshot is not sufficient evidence. Review whether payment accounts, currencies, countries, and onboarding documents align with one another, and flag large or unusually fragmented transactions.</p>
<p>Export weekly spend, compare platform invoices with bank or payment-provider statements, and document discrepancies before taking action. Avoid automatic top-ups while a discrepancy is under investigation, as duplicate charges may result.</p>
<h3>3. Review claims, restrictions, and creative disclosures</h3>
<p>Evaluate ad copy, images, videos, and landing pages by country, industry, and campaign objective. Pay particular attention to absolute claims such as “number one,” “best,” or “100% guaranteed,” as well as performance promises involving health, finance, lotteries, weight loss, or franchise opportunities.</p>
<p>Prices, discount deadlines, inventory availability, and geographic restrictions must remain consistent across the ad and landing page. Keep supporting records for real-person testimonials, before-and-after content, user reviews, and demonstration materials.</p>
<h3>4. Validate landing pages and data collection</h3>
<p>Inspect more than the first screen. Check the domain, page availability, mobile display, redirect paths, form fields, and privacy-policy access. The information requested in a form should match what the advertiser actually collects and should not include sensitive data unrelated to the campaign purpose.</p>
<p>For pages using cookies, tracking parameters, or advertising pixels, document the data purpose, retention period, and available consent-withdrawal process. In regions with stricter privacy expectations, confirm that an appropriate consent-management mechanism is in place.</p>
<h3>5. Analyze rejections, resubmissions, and warnings</h3>
<p>Maintain a rejection log with the platform, date, account, creative URL, reason, supporting documents, and outcome. If similar issues repeat, investigate the account identity, payment chain, or landing page rather than merely changing the headline.</p>
<p>Use three priority levels:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Red:</strong> Account restrictions, unverified entities, or payment irregularities. Pause scaling and investigate immediately.</li>
<li><strong>Yellow:</strong> Rejected creatives or landing pages requiring evidence. Update and resubmit the same day.</li>
<li><strong>Green:</strong> Routine copy or data updates that can wait for the next inspection cycle.</li>
</ul>
<h3>6. Preserve versions and action records</h3>
<p>Retain each creative version, landing-page snapshot, submission timestamp, and review result. Standardize file names—for example, “country—product—format—version”—to prevent teams from uploading the wrong asset or resubmitting duplicate appeals.</p>
<h3>7. Close the weekly loop</h3>
<p>Produce a one-page weekly report covering new risks, completed fixes, missing evidence, abnormal accounts, and next actions. Assign an owner and deadline to recurring issues. Review rejection rates, resubmission counts, and account restrictions the following month to evaluate whether the inspection process is working.</p>
<h2>Editor’s Note: Compliance Is Part of Media Efficiency</h2>
<p>An account that repeatedly requests documentation or recreates ads may still show a positive balance, but its usable budget is already being reduced by review delays. A manual checklist can also become ineffective once a team scales to many accounts.</p>
<p>A more reliable approach is to connect entity records, recharge evidence, creative versions, and rejection reasons in one traceable system. Media, growth, and finance teams can then confirm the fields they each own. Compliance inspection cannot guarantee that an ad will never be rejected, because platform policies evolve. It can, however, ensure that teams know where the evidence is, who owns the correction, and how to avoid repeating the same mistake next time.</p>
